    <article class="prose">
        <h2 id="problem">Why SQL Server Express backups are not automatic</h2>
        <p>On Standard and Enterprise edition you would create a maintenance plan or an Agent job. On Express neither exists: the SQL Server Agent service is not included, and the Maintenance Plans node is missing from SQL Server Management Studio. Unless you add something yourself, an Express database is only backed up when someone remembers to right-click it.</p>

        <h2 id="task-scheduler">The usual workaround: a script in Windows Task Scheduler</h2>
        <p>The common do-it-yourself fix is a batch or PowerShell script that calls <code>sqlcmd</code>, started by Task Scheduler:</p>
        <pre><code>sqlcmd -S .\SQLEXPRESS -E -Q "BACKUP DATABASE [SalesDB] TO DISK = N'D:\Backups\SalesDB.bak' WITH INIT"</code></pre>
        <p>It works &mdash; until it doesn't. A typical script:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>overwrites the same file every night, or fills the disk because nothing deletes old backups;</li>
            <li>never notices that a backup failed, because nobody reads the Task Scheduler history;</li>
            <li>forgets databases that were created after the script was written;</li>
            <li>keeps the only copy on the same server as the database;</li>
            <li>stores a SQL password in plain text when Windows authentication isn't an option.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="solution">The easy way: SQL Server Backup Suite</h2>
        <p>SQL Server Backup Suite replaces SQL Server Agent for backups. Its Windows service runs the schedule, so it works the same on Express as on Enterprise &mdash; and adds what the scripts lack.</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong><a href="/download">Download</a> and install.</strong> The backup service is registered and started for you.</li>
            <li><strong>Add a connection</strong> to your Express instance, usually <code>.\SQLEXPRESS</code> or <code>SERVERNAME\SQLEXPRESS</code>, and click <em>Test connection</em>.</li>
            <li><strong>Click <em>Create dedicated backup login</em></strong> so backups don't depend on the Windows service account (requires mixed-mode authentication), or grant the service account backup rights.</li>
            <li><strong>Create a job</strong>: <em>AllUserDatabases</em>, type <em>Full</em>, <em>Daily</em> at a quiet time, a destination folder, and <em>KeepLastN</em> retention.</li>
            <li><strong>Untick <em>Native backup compression</em></strong> &mdash; Express doesn't support it &mdash; keep verification on, and click <em>Save</em>.</li>
        </ol>
        <screenshot name="job-editor" caption="A daily full backup of every user database, discovered at run time." />
        <p>That's it. New databases are picked up automatically, old backups are cleaned up, every file is verified with <code>RESTORE VERIFYONLY</code>, and the dashboard shows the next and last run of every job.</p>

