        <h2 id="express-tips">SQL Server Express backup tips</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Recovery model:</strong> databases inherit the recovery model of <code>model</code>, which is <em>SIMPLE</em> on Express by default. Switch important databases to <em>FULL</em> if you want transaction log backups and point-in-time restores.</li>
            <li><strong>Compression:</strong> not available on Express. Plan disk space for uncompressed backups, or use the <em>OffsiteOnly</em> storage mode to keep the local disk free.</li>
            <li><strong>Remote Express instances:</strong> enable TCP/IP in SQL Server Configuration Manager and start the SQL Server Browser service for named instances, or connect with <code>host,port</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Folder permissions:</strong> the SQL Server service account (for example <code>NT Service\MSSQL$SQLEXPRESS</code>) writes the backup file and needs write access to the destination.</li>
            <li><strong>Test a restore</strong> on another machine now and then &mdash; a verified backup is good; a restored one is proof.</li>
        </ul>

